    "uid": "scaling-laws/arXiv%3A2401.17139",
    "title": "Diff-eRank: A Novel Rank-Based Metric for Evaluating Large Language Models",
    "citation": "Lai Wei et al., 2024",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.17139",
    "authors": "Lai Wei; Zhiquan Tan; Chenghai Li; Jin-Dong Wang; Weiran Huang",
    "year": "2024",
    "original_source": false
  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures downstream benchmark accuracy across model sizes, a different measure bearing on V6. It does not fit or test the original loss-scaling laws.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports reduced cross-entropy loss (untrained-model loss minus trained-model loss) across model sizes, but not raw held-out loss curves or power-law fits. These reduced-loss results are recorded under fits_no_version rather than as tests of V1. The multimodal eRank alignment results do not measure modality-specific loss scaling.",
    "test_reason": "The paper validates its own Diff-eRank metric against model size. It reads benchmark accuracy and reduced loss of off-the-shelf OPT, Cerebras-GPT and OpenELM checkpoints as reference trends. Its question is whether Diff-eRank tracks those trends, not whether downstream performance scales predictably. It fits no scaling relation, makes no prediction, and draws no conclusion about scaling laws. So the size trends serve as a yardstick, not a test of V6 or any other version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that Diff-eRank increases with model size and correlates with reduced loss and downstream accuracy. They call the occasional outliers normal and say these do not affect the overall trend. They also propose eRank-based alignment metrics and report that contemporary multimodal LLMs align well.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Introduces a representation-based effective-rank metric and compares it with reduced cross-entropy loss and benchmark accuracy; it does not reproduce the original scaling-law fits.",
    "method_sensitivity": "For Diff-eRank, the increasing size trend appears only in the last layer among the first, middle, and last layers tested; the paper reports an increasing trend for both of its dataset aggregation algorithms.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Reduced cross-entropy loss rises with OPT size on four datasets (Table 6) and across OpenELM and Cerebras-GPT on dolly-15k (Tables 7-8). Exceptions: Cerebras-GPT 13B (8.5152) is below 6.7B (8.5339); OPT 13B on piqa (7.068) is below 6.7B (7.077); OPT 13B on openbookqa (6.236) is below 6.7B (6.258).",
        "proposed_claim": "Loss reduction relative to an untrained model tends to grow with model size within public model families, with occasional non-monotonic points at the largest sizes.",
        "row_ids": []
      },
      {
        "finding": "Diff-eRank (effective-rank reduction of last-layer representations) increases with model size. First and middle layers show no increasing trend (Table 4).",
        "proposed_claim": "Representation effective-rank reduction grows with model size at the last layer only.",
        "row_ids": []
      }
    ],
    "controls": "n",
    "capability_check": "n",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "https://github.com/waltonfuture/Diff-eRank",
    "peer_reviewed": "NeurIPS 2024",
    "problems": "The paper reports trends and correlations rather than predictive fits. It notes outliers; uncertainty estimates are not reported for the benchmark results.",
    "rows_left_out": "Reduced-loss values are discussed in fits_no_version rather than included as rows because the study reports the difference between untrained and trained losses, not the held-out loss named in V1. Multimodal eRank/alignment results and training-stage comparisons do not test any listed version.",
    "untested": [
      {
        "version": "V1",
        "parts": "No held-out loss curves or power-law fits. Reduced loss (untrained minus trained) on five to seven public checkpoints only."
      },
      {
        "version": "V2",
        "parts": "No small-run fits or extrapolation."
      },
      {
        "version": "V3",
        "parts": "No compute-allocation analysis."
      },
      {
        "version": "V4",
        "parts": "No architecture-shape comparison at fixed size."
      },
      {
        "version": "V5",
        "parts": "Multimodal part measures eRank alignment, not loss scaling."
      },
      {
        "version": "V6",
        "parts": "Accuracy-vs-size values are reported as reference trends only. There is no predictive fit, no held-out-scale prediction, and no test of predictability."
      },
      {
        "version": "V7",
        "parts": "No analysis of fitting choices or exponents."
      }
    ],
    "missing": [],
    "verdict_notes": "The reader labelled every single-point row 'partial', which is meaningless for an isolated value. The accuracy and reduced-loss numbers are reference metrics for validating Diff-eRank on public checkpoints, so I classed the study as T4 and gave no verdicts. If a curator prefers to treat Tables 1, 7 and 8 as weak T3 evidence on V6, the facts are these. Accuracy rises roughly monotonically for OPT on piqa, OpenELM and Cerebras-GPT. OPT on openbookqa peaks at 2.7B (0.370 vs 0.360 and 0.366). There are no fits or uncertainty estimates."
  },
